# Maps, Streams, And Optional Effects

These examples approve an order and prepare a CSV export. Each Action can
return its result alone or with a list of requests for the caller:

```text
{:ok, map_or_output}
{:ok, map_or_output, requests}
```

The requests in this guide are application data. `Jido.Exec` returns them;
the caller decides how to perform them. No email or audit service is needed
to run this guide. No request wrapper or Jido core dependency is needed.

## Use The Local Package

This example uses the local source with Flow effect support. Before you start
Livebook, set `JIDO_ACTION_PATH` to the absolute path of your `jido_action`
folder. The path must be available to the Livebook runtime. Run the cells in
order.

```elixir
package_path = System.fetch_env!("JIDO_ACTION_PATH")

Mix.install([{:jido_action, path: package_path}], lockfile: Path.join(package_path, "mix.lock"))
```

## Return A Map With An Optional Request

The Action creates an approval result. If `notify` is true, it also requests
an order confirmation. The caller owns the state update and notification.

```elixir
defmodule EffectExamples.ApproveOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "example_approve_order",
    description: "Prepares an order approval and an optional confirmation request",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        order_id: Zoi.string(),
        notify: Zoi.boolean() |> Zoi.default(false)
      }),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), status: Zoi.enum([:approved])})

  @impl true
  def run(%{order_id: order_id, notify: notify}, _context) do
    approval = %{order_id: order_id, status: :approved}

    if notify do
      {:ok, approval, [{:send_confirmation, order_id}]}
    else
      {:ok, approval}
    end
  end
end
```

Run the same Action directly and inside a Flow. The Flow returns the same map
and requests without changes to the Action.

```elixir
alias Jido.Action.Output
alias Jido.{Exec, Flow, Instruction}
alias Jido.Flow.Ref

approval_flow =
  Flow.new!(%{
    name: "approve_order",
    components: [
      %{
        kind: :step,
        name: "approve",
        action: EffectExamples.ApproveOrder,
        params: Ref.input([])
      }
    ],
    output: Ref.result("approve")
  })

for target <- [EffectExamples.ApproveOrder, approval_flow] do
  {:ok, %{order_id: "order-42", status: :approved}} =
    Exec.run(target, %{order_id: "order-42"})

  {:ok, %{order_id: "order-42", status: :approved}, [{:send_confirmation, "order-42"}]} =
    Exec.run(target, %{order_id: "order-42", notify: true})
end
```

Omit the third element when there are no requests. Returning `[]` is also
valid; Exec returns the same two-element success form.

## Return A Stream With An Optional Request

The export Action accepts a row source through context. Each row has integer
`order_id` and `total_cents` fields. It builds a lazy CSV stream and can request
an audit entry that records when the export was prepared.

```elixir
defmodule EffectExamples.ExportOrders do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "example_export_orders",
    description: "Prepares a lazy CSV stream and an optional audit request",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        report_id: Zoi.string(),
        audit: Zoi.boolean() |> Zoi.default(false)
      })

  @impl true
  def run(%{report_id: report_id, audit: audit}, context) do
    case Map.fetch(context, :rows) do
      {:ok, rows} ->
        lines = Stream.map(rows, fn row -> "#{row.order_id},#{row.total_cents}\n" end)
        stream = Stream.concat(["order_id,total_cents\n"], lines)

        output =
          Jido.Action.Output.stream(stream,
            meta: %{content_type: "text/csv", report_id: report_id}
          )

        if audit do
          requests = [{:audit, %{event: :export_prepared, report_id: report_id}}]
          {:ok, output, requests}
        else
          {:ok, output}
        end

      :error ->
        {:error, :missing_sales_rows}
    end
  end
end
```

`Output.meta` describes the stream. The third tuple element contains only
effects. Exec does not read the rows or perform the audit request.

```elixir
export_flow =
  Flow.new!(%{
    name: "export_orders",
    components: [
      %{
        kind: :step,
        name: "export",
        action: EffectExamples.ExportOrders,
        params: Ref.input([])
      }
    ],
    output: Ref.result("export")
  })

rows = [%{order_id: 42, total_cents: 1250}, %{order_id: 43, total_cents: 750}]
context = %{rows: rows}
params = %{report_id: "report-7", audit: true}
expected_csv = "order_id,total_cents\n42,1250\n43,750\n"

for target <- [EffectExamples.ExportOrders, export_flow] do
  {:ok, %Output{kind: :stream, value: stream},
   [{:audit, %{event: :export_prepared, report_id: "report-7"}}]} =
    Exec.run(target, params, context)

  # The caller consumes the stream after Exec has returned.
  ^expected_csv = Enum.join(stream)

  {:ok, %Output{kind: :stream}} =
    Exec.run(target, %{report_id: "report-7"}, context)
end
```

The audit event means **export prepared**. It does not mean that all rows were
read or sent. A row source can fail during later consumption. That failure
cannot revoke a request that Exec has already returned. If the application
needs an audit entry for completed delivery, it must create that entry after
it has consumed and delivered the stream successfully.

The caller must keep the row source available while it consumes the stream.
An Action-owned resource can close when the Action returns.

## Use Instructions

An Instruction gives Exec the target, parameters, and context as one value.

```elixir
instruction = Instruction.new!(target: export_flow, params: params, context: context)
expected_requests = [{:audit, %{event: :export_prepared, report_id: "report-7"}}]

{:ok, %Output{value: stream}, ^expected_requests} = Exec.run(instruction)
^expected_csv = Enum.join(stream)
```

## Handle Failure

Without a row source, the export fails before it creates the stream. Exec
returns an error with no audit request.

```elixir
{:error, _error} = Exec.run(export_flow, params)
```

For a Flow with several Actions, only successful completion returns the full
effect list. Failure, cancellation, and timeout return no partial effect list.
See the [execution contract](execution.md#results-and-errors) for the complete
ordering and failure rules.

## Run The Example Tests

From the `jido_action` folder:

```shell
mix test test/examples/action_effects_test.exs
```

The tests execute this guide's Elixir cells with the current package source.
They also check lazy consumption, optional effects, entry-point agreement,
and failure before and after stream creation. They run in the default suite.
